{
 "node": "Contact",
 "mode": "events",
 "note": "X_ITE 16.6.0: a Contact is a plain data node: the sensor creates a pool of them (the same objects are overwritten every frame) and copies appliedParameters, bounce, minBounceSpeed, frictionCoefficients, surfaceSpeed, softnessConstantForceMix, softnessErrorCorrection from its own collider and slipFactors into slipCoefficients; position (a point on the first body's surface), contactNormal (unit) and depth (>= 0) come from the physics world; frictionDirection is never set (0 1 0). A Contact written into the scene (the schema allows it only among the scene's own nodes, not inside a RigidBodyCollection) or routed to the world's set_contacts changes nothing in the simulation (the handler is empty). Defaults of a bare Contact: minBounceSpeed 0 (the collider's is 0.1), softnessConstantForceMix 0.0001, softnessErrorCorrection 0.8, appliedParameters BOUNCE. body1, body2, geometry1 and geometry2 stay null in X_ITE (a typo: it writes a property with a trailing underscore); the bridge fills them with the two bodies and top-level collidables of the pair, as the specification says.",
 "gaps": [
  "body1, body2, geometry1, geometry2: the bridge fills them, X_ITE leaves them null (checked on each side)",
  "the sign of contactNormal depends on the order of the pair and flips with nested shapes: only its length and axis are asserted",
  "a Contact routed to set_contacts is held and does nothing, as in X_ITE"
 ],
